  "query_preview": "..High pressure aloft was centered over Oklahoma\nearly morning Tuesday with a strong trough centered over the \nPacific Northwest.\n\nFrom Today into Thursday...High pressure will gradually build \nback over Arizona in response to the Pacific Northwest trough\nmoving into the central United States. Monsoon moisture will \ncontinue to linger and even gradually increase through this \nperiod. As a result, the afternoon/evening shower and thunderstorm\nthreat will continue across the high terrain of eastern Arizona \nbut also gradually spread westward along the Mogollon Rim. Any \nheavy rain potential is expected to remain confined to the White \nMountains region. Look for warmer temperatures as the high builds \nwith mountain temperatures climbing to the upper 70s to mid 80s \nreaching 95 to 105 at lower elevations.\n\nFor Friday and beyond...An additional trough is expected to drive\nacross the Pacific Northwest pushing the high center to the east \nof Arizona. It appears monsoon moisture will remain in play and\ninteract with the passing Pacific Northwest trough. The models \nare showing good chances for widespread coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms Friday through Saturday with some level of activity \ncontinuing through the overnight hours. The passing trough will \nresult in increased vertical wind shear with a few storms possibly \nbecoming organized, thus raising the threat of severe \nthunderstorms and localized flash flooding. The trough heads \neastward on Sunday with a drier and more stable air mass moving \nacross most of northern Arizona. Under the influence of the trough\nit will be come breezy with general west to southwest winds at\n10-20 mph gusting to 25 mph starting Friday and lasting through\nthe weekend.",
